id: rust.arc
kind: rust
title: Arc<T>
aliases: arc, atomic reference counting, shared ownership
tags: concurrency, ownership
questions:
  - how do you share data between threads or tasks in Rust?
invariant: Arc shares immutable ownership by atomic reference counting; it is alive until the last handle drops.
answer_30s: Arc<T> shares immutable ownership across threads; mutation needs an interior-mutability wrapper (Mutex, RwLock, atomics) or, better, message passing to a single owner.
answer_2m: |
  All Arc does is keep one value alive until the last clone drops, paying an
  atomic per clone. It does not allow mutation, and Arc<Mutex<T>> is a
  composition where the mutex supplies mutation and the Arc supplies sharing.
  The usual interview trap is treating Arc<Mutex> as a substitute for design: a
  mutex in the middle of a request path serializes that path, so the fix is
  often to restructure ownership (per-task state, a channel to the owner) rather
  than to wrap more in a lock. Use Weak for back edges to avoid cycles.
when_to_use:
  - config or clients shared read-only by many tasks
  - genuinely shared mutable state with a small critical section
avoid_when:
  - one task owns the state (move it) or writes can be queued (use a channel)
minimal_example: |
  use std::sync::Arc;
  use tokio::sync::Mutex;

  #[derive(Clone)]
  struct AppState { db: Arc<DbPool>, cache: Arc<Mutex<HashMap<String, String>>> }

  let state = AppState { db: Arc::clone(&db), cache: Arc::new(Mutex::new(HashMap::new())) };
  tokio::spawn({ let s = state.clone(); async move { let _ = s; } });
failure_modes:
  - Arc<Mutex<T>> around everything, serializing unrelated work
  - an Arc cycle, so nothing ever drops
  - cloning an Arc per call just to avoid a borrow, paying atomics pointlessly
misuse:
  - arc mutex everywhere => hides the real ownership boundary and costs contention
follow_ups:
  - what is the difference between Arc and Rc?
related:
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.rc-vs-arc
  - rust.rwlock
  - rust.send-sync
  - rust.tokio-spawn
  - design.shared-state
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.Arc.html
